We normally only think of Immanuel as a part of Advent and Christmas. But the reality of *God with us* is much greater, and much richer. We can actually see where God has been with humanity all throughout the Old and New Testaments.

### **Personal Presence of God**

Looking at Scripture, from the story of Cain into the Exodus, we can deduce that God principally dealt with humanity one-on-one. We can see this in the examples of Cain (Genesis 4), Enoch (Genesis 5), Noah (Genesis 6-9), Abraham (Genesis 12-25), and Moses (in Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers, and Deuteronomy). We could call these the Personal Presence of God.

### **Dwelling Presence of God**

Then there appears to be a dramatic shift with the building of the Tabernacle. In the Holy of Holies and the Mercy Seat, above the Ark of the Covenant, God starts to reside among His People. However, even living in their midst, the communication between God and the Children of Israel is principally done through the three offices: those of Priest, Prophet, and King. God lived in the midst of His People through the Ark of the Covenant in the Holy of Holies, but He spoke to His People, at various times, through various priests, prophets, and kings. We could call these the Dwelling Presence of God.&nbsp;

### **Incarnate Presence of God**

Then, something amazing happened when Jesus was born. This is the event that we normally associate with Immanuel. The Incarnation happens. God comes into our midst in a whole new way. Not simply individually, as He did before the Tabernacle, and not only at special times through priests, prophets, or kings, but actually living among us. In bodily form.&nbsp;

#### Long ago, at many times and in many ways, God spoke to our fathers by the prophets, but in these last days he has spoken to us by his Son, whom he appointed the heir of all things, through whom also he created the world. (Hebrews 1:1-2)

Throughout the life of Jesus, He lived among men. However, His physical body was limited, like ours, by time and space. Amazingly He was here with us, in our midst, but because of the limitations of time and space, present only with those in His immediate company. We could refer to this as the Incarnate Presence of God.&nbsp;

### **Indwelling Presence of God**

After the crucifixion of Jesus, we could think similarly to how the disciples seemed to have thought, that the time of Immanuel was finished.

#### \

And he [Jesus] said to them, “What is this conversation that you are holding with each other as you walk?” And they stood still, looking sad. Then one of them, named Cleopas, answered him, “Are you the only visitor to Jerusalem who does not know the things that have happened there in these days?” &nbsp;****And he said to them, “What things?” And they said to him, “Concerning Jesus of Nazareth, a man who was a prophet mighty in deed and word before God and all the people, &nbsp;****and how our chief priests and rulers delivered him up to be condemned to death, and crucified him. But we had hoped that he was the one to redeem Israel. Yes, and besides all this, it is now the third day since these things happened.”&nbsp; (Luke 24:17-21)

#### But now I am going to him who sent me, and none of you asks me, ‘Where are you going?’ But because I have said these things to you, sorrow has filled your heart. Nevertheless, I tell you the truth: it is to your advantage that I go away, for if I do not go away, the Helper will not come to you. But if I go, I will send him to you. (John 16:5-7)

\
The disciples are sad and confused. Immanuel, God dwelling among them, has finished! Then something profound happens. The Holy Spirit comes on all of the believers. As it is related in Acts 2, in the sermon that Peter gives immediately after this happens, he remembers Joel 2:28:

#### \

And it shall come to pass afterward, that I will pour out my Spirit on all flesh; your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, and your young men shall see visions.

A new thing, indeed! While the Presence during Christ’s ministry was limited to where He was physically, there is no such physical limitation now! God is with us once again!

&nbsp;

#### &nbsp;****Do you not know that you are God's temple and that God's Spirit dwells in you? (1 Corinthians 3:16)

I have seen the effects of the Holy Spirit in the lives of believers in the U.S., Canada, Brazil, Guinea-Bissau, and in other places. He works through all believers – Immanuel is present within each of us. We could call this the Indwelling Presence of God.&nbsp;

### **Consummate Presence of God**

Tish Harrison Warren, in her book *Advent: The Season of Hope*, calls the Incarnation the Past Advent. This is what we normally think of as Immanuel. She also says that there is a second Advent, and that is when Immanuel, through His Holy Spirit, comes into our individual lives with greater and greater power. He does this through prayer, Scripture, the Sacraments, and through our community of believers. She then states that there is a future Advent. That is when Christ will come again in Glory. As the Nicene Creed says,&nbsp;

#### …and he shall come again, with glory, to judge the living and the dead; and his kingdom will have no end. (Nicene Creed, BCP 2019)

This is the Consummate Immanuel. First Thessalonians 4:17 states that we “will **always** be with the Lord.”&nbsp;

This is the final fulfillment of Immanuel.&nbsp;

In C.S. Lewis’s *The Last Battle*, Jewel, the Unicorn, as he leads the charge into the true Narnia, says:

#### “I have come home at last! This is my real country! I belong here. This is the land I have been looking for all my life, though I never knew it till now. The reason why we loved the old Narnia was that it sometimes looked a little like this. Breeze like this that has come to us now! Come further up, come further in!"

#### Aslan says to Lucy, Peter, and Edmund:\

"The term is over: the holidays have begun. The dream is ended: this is the morning.”

Warren says that this is the true meaning of Advent, the true Immanuel. We are in the “in-between times,” the “here, but not yet” as we wait for the final defeat of death, suffering, injustice, and brokenness. And what is this true Immanuel like? Scripture gives us a glimpse of that morning when the promise of Immanuel reaches its ultimate fulfillment:

#### \

Then I saw a new heaven and a new earth, for the first heaven and the first earth had passed away, and the sea was no more. And I saw the holy city, new Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God, prepared as a bride adorned for her husband. And I heard a loud voice from the throne saying, “Behold, the dwelling place of God is with man. He will dwell with them, and they will be his people, and God himself will be with them as their God. He will wipe away every tear from their eyes, and death shall be no more, neither shall there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain anymore, for the former things have passed away. (Revelation 21:1-4)

We are living in the greatest Presence of God among us to date, but it is not to be compared with the Consummate Presence of God with us! God has always been with us—it is just that His manner of doing this keeps deepening.&nbsp;

### **Immanuel in the Alpha Course**

As we are in the midst of the Indwelling Immanuel, we should want others also to experience what we have. The Alpha Course helps others to experience Immanuel. For example, the video *Is There More to Life Than This?* can create a desire to explore more. *Who is Jesus?* points viewers to Jesus. *Why and How Should I Read the Bible?* can help them discover the God who dwelt with His People. The three videos on the Holy Spirit can introduce the viewers to Immanuel indwelling the hearts of believers. The entire Alpha Course is designed to bring Immanuel to those who don’t yet know Him. Which of your friends and acquaintances would you like to experience Immanuel in all of His fullness? There’s an Alpha for that!
